# Design: Three outcomes for the deep FTS check

## Problem
ohya doctor --deep reported each error as "events_fts failed the FTS5
integrity-check", with the remedy to restore the database from a
backup. The check copies the database to a temporary file under TMPDIR
first. A full disk or a missing TMPDIR therefore produced the same
finding as a corrupt index. The help text and four documents also said
that the check runs in memory.

## Approach
The finding reports what happened. checkFTSIndex measures the free
space under TMPDIR with the rule that ohya setup uses for a recovery
point, and refuses with the bytes that the check needs and the bytes
that are free. An error that wraps ErrFTSIndexInconsistent keeps the
remedy to restore a backup. Each other error reports that the deep
check could not run, with the cause.

## Key Trade-offs
- One space rule for both callers: MeasureCopySpace in internal/setup.
  The copy needs the database, its WAL, 10 percent, and a 64 MiB
  margin.
- The guard reads the sizes before the copy. A disk that fills during
  the copy still gives "could not run" with the SQLite error, not the
  remedy to restore a backup.
- The hub keeps running during the check: WAL mode and one read
  snapshot (commit 3bf3d50).

## Evidence
Live hub, 2026-11-24: an 18.1 GB database, 24 min 45 s, 78 GiB free
after the run, fts_index ok.

